# Command gitleaks

## What does this command do:

Scans git repositories, directories, files, and stdin for hardcoded secrets — API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys — in both current content and full git history.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: sudo dnf install gitleaks
For Raspberry Pi install using:
```bash
# Debian Trixie's apt package is 8.16.0 (old detect/protect CLI) — use the
# upstream release binary instead so the syntax matches the other three hosts.
VER=8.30.1
curl -sSfL "https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_linux_arm64.tar.gz" \
  | tar -xz -C /tmp gitleaks
install -m 0755 /tmp/gitleaks ~/.local/bin/gitleaks
gitleaks version
```
For Mac install using: brew install gitleaks
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: sudo dnf install gitleaks

> Note: the Mac container runs Fedora Linux on ARM (aarch64), not x86_64. When a
> package is not installed via `dnf`, download the `aarch64`/`arm64` build of the
> binary — never the `x86_64`/`amd64` build used on the Fedora workstation.
> gitleaks is packaged by Fedora itself, so `dnf` covers both arches here.

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/gitleaks
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/.local/bin/gitleaks
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/gitleaks
For Mac container run: /usr/bin/gitleaks

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks

## Command options

# Show version
gitleaks version

# Scan the git history of the repo in the current directory
gitleaks git .

# Scan the git history of a repo elsewhere
gitleaks git /path/to/repo

# Scan only staged changes — use this in a pre-commit hook
gitleaks git --staged .

# Scan only the working-tree diff (uncommitted changes)
gitleaks git --pre-commit .

# Scan a commit range using git log options
gitleaks git --log-opts="HEAD~5..HEAD" .

# Generate clickable links for a forge
gitleaks git --platform github .

# Scan a directory or single file WITHOUT using git history
gitleaks dir /path/to/dir
gitleaks dir ./config.yaml

# Follow symlinks while scanning a directory
gitleaks dir --follow-symlinks /path/to/dir

# Scan piped input
cat some_file | gitleaks stdin

# Redact secrets from output (100% by default; partial with a percentage)
gitleaks git --redact .
gitleaks git --redact=20 .

# Write a report — formats: json, csv, junit, sarif, template
gitleaks git . --report-format json --report-path leaks.json
gitleaks dir . --report-format sarif --report-path leaks.sarif

# Verbose output — prints each finding as it is discovered
gitleaks git -v .

# Use a specific config file
gitleaks git -c .gitleaks.toml .
# Config precedence: --config/-c > $GITLEAKS_CONFIG > $GITLEAKS_CONFIG_TOML > (target)/.gitleaks.toml > built-in

# Run only specific rules by id
gitleaks git --enable-rule=aws-access-token,github-pat .

# Ignore known/accepted findings via a baseline file
gitleaks git . --report-format json --report-path baseline.json   # create it
gitleaks git . --baseline-path baseline.json                      # then scan against it

# Point at a .gitleaksignore file (fingerprints of accepted findings)
gitleaks git -i /path/to/.gitleaksignore .

# Honour or ignore inline "gitleaks:allow" comments
gitleaks git --ignore-gitleaks-allow .

# Skip files larger than N megabytes
gitleaks git --max-target-megabytes 10 .

# Recurse into archives and encoded blobs (both default to 0 = off)
gitleaks dir --max-archive-depth 2 --max-decode-depth 2 .

# Quiet the banner / color / log level
gitleaks git --no-banner --no-color -l error .

# Change the exit code used when leaks are found (default 1; 0 = never fail)
gitleaks git . --exit-code 0

# Fail the scan if it runs longer than N seconds
gitleaks git . --timeout 300

# Shell completion (zsh)
gitleaks completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_gitleaks"

# Suppress a single false positive — append to the offending line:
#   my_key = "AKIA..." # gitleaks:allow

# Suppress by fingerprint — put the fingerprint from the report into .gitleaksignore:
#   echo '<commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>' >> .gitleaksignore

# Use as a pre-commit hook (.git/hooks/pre-commit)
#   #!/usr/bin/env bash
#   gitleaks git --staged --redact --no-banner . || {
#     echo "gitleaks blocked this commit"; exit 1;
#   }

# NOTE: 'gitleaks detect' and 'gitleaks protect' are the deprecated pre-8.19 names
# for 'gitleaks git' and 'gitleaks git --staged'. Debian's apt package (8.16.0)
# still uses the old names — that is why the Pi install above uses the upstream binary.
